f we zoom in on  the center you can start seeing what are  called photo sites that come together to  make pixels on this sensor now for the  techniques that we're going to be using  throughout this tutorial series the only  requirement is that you have a camera  and that that camera can change settings  those settings are the shutter speed  often represented with a fraction of a  second that ISO which is a number  usually between 100 and 10,000 but it  can go on either end of that and the  aperture most DSLRs and point-and-shoot  should have this functionality built in  you may have to go to a mode called  manual on a cell phone some phones have  the ability to change these settings  just out of the box they have their apps  are able to sometimes you need to  download a different app that will give  you access to these settings the one  thing to note about whatever app you use  if you're going to use a cell phone you  cannot adjust aperture on a cell phone  now that you have the ability to change  your camera settings we're going to talk  about what all of these do and  essentially all of these control how  much light enters your camera the more  of those photons those little pieces of  light that reach your sensor the  brighter your image is going to be and  so we have three different ways this is  often called the exposure triangle to  adjust this amount of light we have ISO  aperture and shutter and well why do we  have three different ways to do the same  thing each one has a secondary effect on  the image so they don't just change the  brightness of the image they change  something else about the image that  we're going to explore so the first  setting we're going to take a dive into  is the shutter speed now the shutter is  a piece of thin material it's kind of  like a  vinyl plastic material that can open and  close and either let light in or block  light from hitting the center of your  camera so let's see how this works the  shutter speed is measured in fractions  of a second so for example if we had a  shutter speed of 1 over 30 that means  that the shutter is open letting photons  into the center for one thirtieth of a  second and you can have shutter speeds  usually ranging between 30 seconds and  1/8 thousandths of a second on many  cameras and some cameras can go even  longer or shorter the longer the shutter  speed the more photons enter your camera  and the brighter your image is going to  be the shorter your shutter speed the  fewer photons are going to enter your  camera and the darker the image is going  to be if you're taking a picture with a  cell phone it doesn't have a mechanical  shutter it doesn't have these pieces of  plastic that move in front of the center  it's too small and so what a cell phone  will do is in order to take a picture  with a cell phone you need to be able to  see the image before you take it there's  a live feed you can see the light coming  in to the camera but in order for you to  see a live picture before you take the  pictures you can see what you're trying  to image you need to capture photons and  compute do computations on that data and  put it on the screen very quickly the  camera is not fast enough the cell phone  is not fast enough to do that all at  once so what the cell phone actually  does is it scans and it's going to scan  line by line so it's going to record the  data where this green line is and it's  gonna start displaying it as it scans  the next line and it's going to scan and  display scan and display and it's gonna  go like this and just like the the DSLR  the faster this happens the less light  your camera records if you have a slow  shutter speed it's gonna record light  for a longer period of time on each line  of that center and you're going to get a  brighter image so what can you do with  shutter speed that sets it apart from  the other ways of controlling how much  light enters your camera what we're  trying to make observations during this  program right an imaging science so let  me show you what you can do so for  example on the left side of the screen  these two images here were taken with a  fast shutter speed or in other words a  very short shutter speed may be around a  thousandth of a second this means it  freezes the motion because the can  picture was taken very quickly you can  freeze what's happening so as this car  moves across the screen here I'm able to  capture it so it looks like it's staying  still same thing with this this carnival  ride in the picture on the bottom but  you'll note is with this fast shutter  speed I don't capture much light so if  you look at this building next to next  to the ride here it's very dark so I may  want to lengthen that shutter speed so  may want to have it open for a longer  period of time and what that can do is  it'll blur your image but it'll show  motion because the cameras going to be  collecting photons while something is  moving so in the first example maybe I  want to show the motion of the car so  this is a long shutter speed this is  probably around a tenth of a second and  as the car moves across it bores so  you're capturing the car in lots of  different positions but it shows the  path really well same thing with the  carnival ride on the bottom now we can  see the motion from the first one you  may not know that this is a spinning  ride but in the second one it's very  clear because it's blurred out in this  circle that this is spinning very  quickly  you'll also notice that this building to  the side is much brighter and again  that's because we've allowed the camera  to capture more photons when that  shutter is open the next setting that  we're going to talk about is aperture  now aperture is the one setting that you  cannot control on a cell phone but on  most other cameras you do have control  of this feature the aperture is  essentially the hole that the light can  travel through in your camera so in your  in your lens you usually have these kind  of circles and this is gonna open up  different amounts depending on your  aperture setting the wider your aperture  is like the one on the left we're gonna  let more photons into your camera and  the one on the right here has a much  smaller hole so that's gonna block most  of the photons so this is going to be a  much darker image with the smaller  aperture let's see how the aperture  works  so besides controlling how much light  comes in the camera why would we use  aperture to control brightness well just  like shutter speed controlled how you  can show movement or freeze movement in  an image aperture has another ability  where it allows you to control what's  called the depth of field this is how  much is in focus when you take your  picture as you can see in these pictures  below with a wide aperture so a very  open aperture here only a small amount  of our picture is actually in focus as  we close down this aperture as we make  the hole smaller more and more of the  picture is going to be in focus and this  is going to let us do things such as  call attention to certain parts of the  image if you can control how much of it  is in focus you can highlight something  by putting it in focus and blurring out  the things that maybe aren't as  important to the image or if you want to  show if everything in the image is  important for what you're trying trying  to show then you might want to use a  smaller aperture and keep everything in  focus aperture is measured in what is  called F stocks or F numbers so the F  numbers work by set going lower numbers  so F 1 point 8 for example a low number  is a wide aperture the higher the number  such as F 32 is a smaller aperture the  reason that these numbers seem a little  bit reversed to what you may think they  should be is that they represent a ratio  between the focal length of a lens  essentially how zoomed in the lens is  and how wide the lenses so now let's  take a quick look at how the aperture  works in terms of changing the depth of  field so let's say we want to take a  picture of this puppy here and so light  is going to come from the puppy and go  to the sensor  in between that the subject and the  camera in the center is the lens so what  the lens is going to do is take parallel  lines of light that come into the camera  it's going to focus them onto what's  called a focal point so all of these  lines of light are going to cross at a  certain point but the lens can only do  this it'll only focus light to the same  point if it comes in parallel to itself  if we have light let's say that comes in  at a different angle this light is not  going to be bent to the exact same point  the aperture is going to basically  control what angle the light can enter  the camera when light doesn't come in  parallel to itself it's no longer  focusing at the same point and it's  going to be blurry so we'll remove the  lens just to make this a little bit  simpler and we'll look at the aperture  so the aperture is you said it's just  the hole that the light is going to  enter the camera through and so when we  have a small aperture like this light is  coming from our image from all different  directions here but with a small  aperture only light that is parallel or  at a very small angle is able to pass  through and get to the sensor all this  other light that's coming in from  different angles is going to get blocked  by that aperture so since all the light  coming through is parallel it's all  going to get focused to the same spot  and no matter where it came from in our  image here it's all going to come in to  the camera at the same focus so we have  an image where it has high depth of  field everything is in focus now let's  say instead we have a very wide aperture  when we have a wide aperture the light  that's going to come into the camera it  doesn't have to be parallel some of it's  going to be parallel but we're also  to allow light in that's coming from all  sorts of different angles because we're  allowing that light into the camera it's  not going to focus the same way as it  passes through the lens your cameras  only going to be able to focus on some  of this light basically light that's  only come from a certain area of this  photo so let's say we want to focus on  his nose then all of the light that's  coming in a straight line from his nose  that's gonna be able to be focused but  like coming back here from his ear  that's coming in at a slightly different  angle it's still now reaching the center  because we have a wide aperture but it's  not going to be in focus it's gonna be a  bit blurry and that's how we're gonna  get that it blurry background or  foreground or whatever you're trying to  do in your photo the third and final  setting that we're going to talk about  in our camera is what's called ISO ISO  used to be ISO standing for  international standards organization  used to be a standard measurement of  what was called film speed essentially  how sensitive was the film to light in  today's camera whether it's a cell phone  or DSLR or any digital camera it's going  to measure the sensitivity of the sensor  and it's no longer standardized so  different ISO numbers are going to match  up to different effects and we'll talk a  little bit about that in a minute  but basically the lower the ISO so let's  say we have an ISO of 100 so if we have  an ISO of 100 this is going to require a  lot more photons in order to get the  same brightness of image then if you  took a picture at 1000 ISO so you need  fewer photons to get the same brightness  in an image because the Centers it's  more sensitive to that light or it's  amplifying the light more with the  higher ISO now this sounds great  well why don't I just control how bright  my images using ISO and just like  shutter speed and aperture ISO has its  own effects on an image so if we take a  look at these images these are four  images taken with all the same settings  except for different ISOs if I put on  the labels you'll see the upper left  corner is at 100 so this is a very dark  image because there was only a few  photons coming to the camera a few  meaning millions when we're talking  about photons were talking about  millions billions of photons entering  the camera but this had a relatively  small number of photons entering the  camera so it's not a very right image if  we boost the sensitivity if we raise the  ISO to let's say a thousand you can see  the image is becoming brighter it  actually captured the same number of  photons in both images because we kept  the aperture to the same the shutter the  same so you're starting to see more  detail we keep going here's iso 5000  it's even brighter we can see a lot more  detail in the image but you're also  starting to see some graininess in the  image and this is really pronounced in  this image at ISO 10000 you can see a  lot of graininess and this is called  noise basically what this is is that  we're getting most of our photons from  the image from the same points in space  but there are some random photons that  are entering the camera and because  we're capturing very little light  remember that at ISO 100 for this image  we really can't see much so if we have a  stray photon a random photon come into  our camera we're going to detect that  because we don't have all the other  photons from our image from this  fireplace here to cancel that out  another way to say that is we have a  very low signal to noise ratio so  there's not much signal  light from the subject that we want to  take a picture of so the noise the  randomness is very easy to see where if  you use a low ISO and if you have a lot  of light a lot of light coming in the  signal-to-noise ratio is very high so  you have a lot of photons from your  subject so the relatively few photons  that are coming in from random sources  aren't going to appear so to wrap up  let's go back to our exposure triangle  and there are three settings that we can  control on a cell phone - and they're  all related to each other they all  control how much light comes into your  image so how do you decide what you're  going to do so it depends on what you're  trying to show in your photo if the  motion or the amount of motion and your  image is really important whether you  want to freeze your image you're gonna  want to pick a shutter speed that gives  you the effect you want and then adjust  the other two ISO an aperture to get the  brightness that you need if the  important part of your photo is the  depth of field so what's in focus what's  not in focus  then you're gonna set your aperture  first and then adjust the other two to  give you the correct exposure for your  image now this triangle is great and it  lets you do all sorts of really cool  things but there's one thing that's  really easy to forget in photography  even though we talked about all the time  and that's light right everything we're  talking about is how much light is  entering your camera and if you're  trying to take a photo and you're not  able to get the effect you want maybe  you want a high depth of field and you  want a fast shutter speed but your image  is getting very grainy because you have  to use a high ISO well what can you do  well how about get more light right  so you can always control the light or  in most cases control the light that's  around you so using a flashlight ceiling  lights the light from the window  cell phone light laser pointer flash  whatever you have around you use light  to copy  say what for what you want to do with  this exposure triangle now let's try a  couple of examples and just see what we  would do for camera settings now camera  settings are gonna vary for every camera  that you try to use so these are just  going to be rough estimates so for  example I want to take a picture of this  bird here so this bird it's flying in  the air so it's moving so what am I  going to prioritize so movements  involved so I'm gonna pick in this case  shutter speed so what I want to use for  this photo is a short shutter speed or  fast shutter speed so I'm going to do a  short shutter speed and that's gonna  mean my image is going to be dark so I  need to figure out well what are the  other things I can adjust well I don't  really care about the background detail  I'm taking this picture to capture the  bird so if I don't need the background  to be in focus I can use a wide aperture  or a low f-stop number and that will  blur out the background but it also give  me more light and then what that leaves  me with is the ISO and I can adjust that  to get the exposure I need based on how  much sunlight there is if there's cloud  or a bunch of other factors with the  light so again for this image short  shutter speed to capture the motion a  wide aperture to compensate for that  lack of light and then I adjusted the  ISO to get the image that I wanted so  let's look at a second example in this  example I have a close-up image of a  dime now as you move closer to your  subject one thing that you're gonna  discover is it is harder to get  everything in focus your depth of field  decreases as you move closer to the  image and that has to do with the angle  the lights entering your camera in our  next tutorial we're going to  specifically talk about close-up imaging  or macro photography um but for now if I  want to take a picture with the entire  diamond focus well which setting am I  going to focus on well I'm worried about  depth of field so it's going to be  aperture so what I want to use here is a  small aperture in order to get that  depth of field or a high f-stop number  if I'm going to have a small aperture  that means that my image is going to be  very dark because it's not letting light  in so that maybe then I'm going to  choose a long shutter speed in order to  compensate for that and then again I'm  going to adjust the ISO in order to get  the exposure I need so in this image  depth of field was the priority so I  chose the aperture and then adjusted the  other settings accordingly thank you for  joining us for this tutorial in our  imaging science series with the MIT  Museum and Koch Institute we'd love to  see your stunning science photos if  you'd like to share them with us you can  show them on Facebook Twitter and  Instagram and use the hashtag MIT em  imaging science to share those with us  at the end of this program so on June  20th will be holding a webinar to look  at what makes a great science photo and  some of those submissions have a chance  to be featured during that webinar for  more information on the imaging science  program other tutorials and other  information and resources please visit  the MIT Museum website at the link below  and you will find a list of our other  resources as part of the Imaging Science  program

