fferent ISOs if I put on  the labels you'll see the upper left  corner is at 100 so this is a very dark  image because there was only a few  photons coming to the camera a few  meaning millions when we're talking  about photons were talking about  millions billions of photons entering  the camera but this had a relatively  small number of photons entering the  camera so it's not a very right image if  we boost the sensitivity if we raise the  ISO to let's say a thousand you can see  the image is becoming brighter it  actually captured the same number of  photons in both images because we kept  the aperture to the same the shutter the  same so you're starting to see more  detail we keep going here's iso 5000  it's even brighter we can see a lot more  detail in the image but you're also  starting to see some graininess in the  image and this is really pronounced in  this image at ISO 10000 you can see a  lot of graininess and this is called  noise basically what this is is that  we're getting most of our photons from  the image from the same points in space  but there are some random photons that  are entering the camera and because  we're capturing very little light  remember that at ISO 100 for this image  we really can't see much so if we have a  stray photon a random photon come into  our camera we're going to detect that  because we don't have all the other  photons from our image from this  fireplace here to cancel that out  another way to say that is we have a  very low signal to noise ratio so  there's not much signal  light from the subject that we want to  take a picture of so the noise the  randomness is very easy to see where if  you use a low ISO and if you have a lot  of light a lot of light coming in the  signal-to-noise ratio is very high so  you have a lot of photons from your  subject so the relatively few photons  that are coming in from random sources  aren't going to appear so to wrap up  let's go back to our exposure triangle  and there are three settings that we can  control on a cell phone - and they're  all related to each other they all  control how much light comes into your  image so how do you decide what you're  going to do so it depends on what you're  trying to show in your photo if the  motion or the amount of motion and your  image is really important whether you  want to freeze your image you're gonna  want to pick a shutter speed that gives  you the effect you want and then adjust  the other two ISO an aperture to get the  brightness that you need if the  important part of your photo is the  depth of field so what's in focus what's  not in focus  then you're gonna set your aperture  first and then adjust the other two to  give you the correct exposure for your  image now this triangle is great and it  lets you do all sorts of really cool  things but there's one thing that's  really easy to forget in photography  even though we talked about all the time  and that's light right everything we're  talking about is how much light is  entering your camera and if you're  trying to take a photo and you're not  able to get the effect you want maybe  you want a high depth of field and you  want a fast shutter speed but your image  is getting very grainy because you have  to use a high ISO well what can you do  well how about get more light right  so you can always control the light or  in most cases control the light that's  around you so using a flashlight ceiling  lights the light from the window  cell phone light laser pointer flash  whatever you have around you use light  to copy  say what for what you want to do with  this exposure triangle now let's try a  couple of examples and just see what we  would do for camera settings now camera  settings are gonna vary for every camera  that you try to use so these are just  going to be rough estimates so for  example I want to take a picture of this  bird here so this bird it's flying in  the air so it's moving so what am I  going to prioritize so movements  involved so I'm gonna pick in this case  shutter speed so what I want to use for  this photo is a short shutter speed or  fast shutter speed so I'm going to do a  short shutter speed and that's gonna  mean my image is going to be dark so I  need to figure out well what are the  other things I can adjust well I don't  really care about the background detail  I'm taking this picture to capture the  bird so if I don't need the background  to be in focus I can use a wide aperture  or a low f-stop number and that will  blur out the background but it also give  me more light and then what that leaves  me with is the ISO and I can adjust that  to get the exposure I need based on how  much sunlight there is if there's cloud  or a bunch of other factors with the  light so again for this image short  shutter speed to capture the motion a  wide aperture to compensate for that  lack of light and then I adjusted the  ISO to get the image that I wanted so  let's look at a second example in this  example I have a close-up image of a  dime now as you move closer to your  subject one thing that you're gonna  discover is it is harder to get  everything in focus your depth of field  decreases as you move closer to the  image and that has to do with the angle  the lights entering your camera in our  next tutorial we're going to  specifically talk about close-up imaging  or macro photography um but for now if I  want to take a picture with the entire  diamond focus well which setting am I  going to focus on well I'm worried about  depth of field so it's going to be  aperture so what I want to use here is a  small aperture in order to get that  depth of field or a high f-stop number  if I'm going to have a small aperture  that means that my image is going to be  very dark because it's not letting light  in so that maybe then I'm going to  choose a long shutter speed in order to  compensate for that and then again I'm  going to adjust the ISO in order to get  the exposure I need so in this image  depth of field was the priority so I  chose the aperture and then adjusted the  other settings accordingly thank you for  joining us for this tutorial in our  imaging science series with the MIT  Museum and Koch Institute we'd love to  see your stunning science photos if  you'd like to share them with us you can  show them on Facebook Twitter and  Instagram and use the hashtag MIT em  imaging science to share those with us  at the end of this program so on June  20th will be holding a webinar to look  at what makes a great science photo and  some of those submissions have a chance  to be featured during that webinar for  more information on the imaging science  program other tutorials and other  information and resources please visit  the MIT Museum website at the link below  and you will find a list of our other  resources as part of the Imaging Science  program
 

